@page id="p6-triune" rh="BUILDING THE EMPIRE · I · THE TRIUNE RULE" m="TKC: TL · TR · CR"
# Three bodies, one score
In 2051 the Crown, the Temple and the Broadcast were made the three governing bodies of the Empire. What had worked informally since 2043 became constitutional, and the Broadcast stopped being a network of Coalition media companies and became a branch of government (TL).
<div class="org">
<div class="or"><div class="on">The Crown<span>House of Vane · Phase Two · 30%</span></div><div class="on">The Temple<span>Sanctum · Vowed · Phase One · 30%</span></div><div class="on pink">The Broadcast<span>Citadel · Saint Meridian · 40%</span></div></div>
<div class="ol"></div>
<div class="or"><div class="on sm">Governor-Prelates<span>one per Dominion</span></div><div class="on sm">The Registry<span>every citizen's paper</span></div><div class="on sm">The Vowed<span>assessment · observation</span></div><div class="on sm">Covenant Court<span>the law, interpreted</span></div></div>
</div>
rec:
How the Trials are split | The Temple runs Phase One and the Crown runs Phase Two. Endurance belongs to the terrain. The Covenant Score is weighted 30 percent Temple, 30 percent Crown and 40 percent Broadcast (TR).
The secret | The Broadcast's share is never disclosed, because the public would be angry to learn that a broadcaster outweighs the Church in choosing the Empire's most powerful woman (TR).
prompt: Who in the cast knows the forty percent, and how they found out | fill

@page id="p6-crown" rh="BUILDING THE EMPIRE · II · THE CROWN AND THE HOUSE OF VANE" m="TKC: TL · AM · RULING · FR 35 · FR 39 · FR 41 · CR · MEN · WR"
# Four rulers, three methods
Every Emperor descends from Elias Vane, who founded the House in 2054 (TL). The Temple teaches that God chose Elias and that the bloodline carries the anointing forward, so the family's status is theological as well as political (CR).
tabrl:
| Ruler | Years | How he held power | Source |
| Elias Vane | First Elector 2043, Emperor 2054–2061 | Belief. A preacher who made people want what he wanted. | TL, AM |
| Crest Vane | 2062–2085 | Procedure: quotas, designations, criminal categories and the Trials | TL |
| Dorian Vane | 2085 to now, 59 in 2104 | Performance. He brought back the spectacle and made the Trials the year's biggest event. | TL, Ruling |
| Judah Elijah Vane | Crown Heir, 25 in 2104 | He hasn't yet. He was raised by the Temple as much as by his parents. | TL |
rec:
Succession | By survivorship and the absence of any other recognized claimant (FR 39). Crest inherited after his elder sibling was removed in 2061. Dorian, that sibling's son, inherited after Crest died (FR 35, 39). When Azrael was declared dead in 2100, the line passed to Judah (TL).
The heir's marriage | A matter of state. Dorian has pushed Judah toward an Anointed Wife since he turned 23, because an unmarried heir is an heir whose loyalties are still unwritten (CR). Elite men usually marry between 25 and 35. The Trials exist partly because of Judah's reluctance, to make the choice feel like an honor instead of pressure (MEN).
Advisory access | Young members of the House take part in "Crown advisory access periods," which is how Azrael got legitimate access to the east wing archive (TL, FR 41).
Lyris Vane | Empress, 58, and the most sophisticated political operator in the Empire. She's spent thirty years performing perfect Anointed Wifehood while running the Crownward's domestic and diplomatic machinery, and lobbied through Dorian for a decade to shape the Anointed Wife's legal protections, framing every gain as his gift (CR). She came from Mountain Dominion (WR).
small: SUPERSEDED: The Crownward file calls Crest Dorian's father. Under FR 35, Crest is Dorian's uncle.
prompt: How did Lyris, a woman from Mountain, become Dorian's wife? What did the Trials have to do with it? | fill

@page id="p6-crownward" rh="BUILDING THE EMPIRE · III · THE CROWNWARD · 1 OF 2" m="TKC: CR; RESEARCH: ARLINGTON, VIRGINIA"
# The capital
The Crownward is built over Washington, D.C., formally established in 2043. Elias occupied the old capital instead of moving it, because you don't abandon the seat of the thing you claim to restore. Most people in 2104 have never seen a photograph of Washington (CR). The Potomac is now the Covenant River, and it divides the city.
<div class="map">
<div class="bank w"><div class="bl">THE SOVEREIGN SIDE · WEST</div><div class="pl hi">Crown Palace</div><div class="pl">Temple Sanctum</div><div class="pl">Broadcast Citadel<br><span>north edge, sees both</span></div><div class="pl long">Sovereign Boulevard<br><span>half a mile, Palace to river</span></div><div class="pl long">Covenant Row<br><span>the twelve Covenant Houses</span></div></div>
<div class="river"><div class="rv">COVENANT RIVER</div><div class="br b1">Sovereign Bridge</div><div class="br b2">Covenant Bridge</div><div class="br b3 pink">Grace Bridge</div></div>
<div class="bank e"><div class="bl">THE ADMINISTRATIVE SIDE · EAST</div><div class="pl hi">The Registry</div><div class="pl">Covenant Court offices</div><div class="pl">Registration bureaus</div><div class="pl">Vowed training</div><div class="pl long">Staff housing · market streets<br><span>Temple schools · Dominion kitchens</span></div></div>
</div>
rec:
The bridges | The Sovereign Bridge is the widest and appears on the currency. The Covenant Bridge is narrower and used by staff. The Grace Bridge is the oldest, never fully renovated, and has blind spots in Broadcast coverage, so it's where people go when they don't want to be filmed.
RESEARCH | In the real city central Washington sits on the Potomac's east bank, and the west bank is Arlington, Virginia. Read literally, the Sovereign Side stands where Arlington National Cemetery and the Pentagon are now. POSSIBLE: Somebody in the Crownward knows the Crown Palace is built over the old national cemetery.

@page rh="BUILDING THE EMPIRE · III · THE CROWNWARD · 2 OF 2" m="TKC: CR"
# The order nobody writes down
The Crownward has a rigid social order that nobody writes down and everybody enforces (CR).
rec:
The House of Vane | At the top.
The Anointed Court | About forty families whose standing is measured by how close they live to the Palace. They attend quarterly audiences with the Emperor, and the Temple High Prelates sit among them.
The Administrative Class | The senior staff of the Crown, Temple and Broadcast. Most of their wives hold Stewardwife designation.
Their daughters | The Crownward's daughters who enter the Trials are rarely the most celebrated candidates, and they're almost always the most dangerous (CR).
prompt: What someone on the Grace Bridge can do that they can't do anywhere else | fill
prompt: A walk across the Crownward, west to east, in Eden's first week | fill

@page id="p6-temple" rh="BUILDING THE EMPIRE · IV · THE TEMPLE AND THE VOWED" m="TKC: DR3 · CR · DOM · MEN · TR · TL · FR 30 · FR 33"
# Ministers, Vowed and the Prelate
The Temple is a church and a branch of government. In Eden's world it shows up as ministers, Vowed women and doctrine. Its scripture is read from the Book of Covenant (DR3), and the Temple Sanctum in the Crownward is its seat (CR).
rec:
Ministers | Senior Minister Bell is the model: a man who signs Binding certificates, sponsors Assessment candidates, and prays God will close every door not intended for a girl (DR3).
The Vowed | Women in Temple service, who run most of the Empire's contact with women. A Vowed officer assesses every Aurem girl at eighteen (DOM). They conduct the quarterly household assessments in Aurem's Commonwife homes (MEN), run the Regional Assessments, and observe, examine and interview the Covenant Daughters (TR). In Red Earth they attend school performances as "pastoral care" (DOM).
The Crownward Vowed | The Temple's elite and the Empire's most efficient intelligence network. The Temple calls it spiritual discernment and the Crown uses it as surveillance, and it's never written down anywhere the Empire could confiscate it (CR).
Leaving | The designation can be left through marriage. Delphine's mother went from Vowed to Bonded Wife, and Hannah was Vowed before she became House Mother (TR, FR 30). Sera Callum was Vowed when Elias married her (TL). Hannah's daughter Naomi has one year left in Vowed education (TL).
@page rh="BUILDING THE EMPIRE · IV · THE TEMPLE AND THE VOWED, CONTINUED" m="TKC: DR3 · CR · DOM · MEN · TR · TL · FR 30 · FR 33"
# The Prelate
cols:
plate: frame | PORTRAIT · THE PRELATE · WORKING NAME AURELIA | 3in
/end
||
k: The Prelate
She heads the Crownward's Vowed. She's 67, has served three Emperors, and knows better than anyone in the city how power actually works. She assessed Judah's spiritual formation personally. In the Trial's first week she'll reach a conclusion about Eden that she shares with no one, the Emperor included. She anoints the Crown Heir's bride (CR).
small: The files call her Prelate Naomi. FR 33 renames her so Hannah's daughter keeps the name. POSSIBLE: Prelate Aurelia is the working name. She is never Huldah.
/end
prompt: What the Prelate concludes about Eden in the first week, and what she does with it | fill

@page id="p6-registry" rh="BUILDING THE EMPIRE · V · THE BROADCAST, THE GOVERNORS, THE REGISTRY" m="TKC: CR · DOM · TR · TL · DR3 · MEN"
# Paper and cameras
rec:
The Broadcast | State media and the third branch. Its Citadel stands in the Crownward, and its studios, where Trial scores are announced, sit in old Midtown Manhattan in Saint Meridian (DOM). It holds 40 percent of the Covenant Score, including "narrative priority decisions" by the Broadcast Director. It edits the Civic Debates for Dominion release and builds a story around every candidate in real time. When a candidate's intelligence becomes the story instead of her compliance, it runs the "Eve narrative," which implies and never accuses (TR).
Governor-Prelates | One political and religious office per Dominion (TL). They choose each Covenant Daughter from the top three of the Regional Assessment, and in practice take whoever the Crown has quietly signaled it prefers (TR). Gulf's first lasted fourteen months. Lone's has always been born in Texas. Pacific's was appointed by Dorian. Ardenne's has mostly stopped trying to fix the marriage records from outside Ashford (DOM).
The Registry | On the Administrative Side. Every citizen's Imperial record: birth designations, marriage registrations, Covenant Scores, Designation results and deaths. Only the Crown Palace is guarded more heavily. Whoever controls what the Registry says about you controls something more basic than law, faith or the Broadcast, and Eden will understand this before most people in the story (CR).
The Covenant Court | Interprets Imperial law, with offices on the Administrative Side and secondary chambers in Blessed Shore, where the law "gets interpreted, refined, and weaponized" (CR, DOM).
Surveillance | Cameras and screens everywhere in the seat cities. The Covenant Houses are wired in every common area but not the bedrooms, a deliberate illusion of privacy that keeps candidates manageable (CR). Pacific has more cameras per block than any other Dominion. Eden's file reads "Exceptional intellect. Monitor closely," later "Selected for Trial. Monitor closely" (TL).
The gaps | The Grace Bridge, the Ardenne hollows, the Sable desert, west Lone's distances, Saint Meridian's density, and the Mountain terrain where Endurance runs mostly without cameras (CR, DOM, TR).
prompt: Who runs the Registry, and who has ever changed what it says about a person | fill

@page rh="BUILDING THE EMPIRE · V · THE LAW ARRIVES AS FORMS" m="TKC: DR3 · TL · MEN · TR · CR · DOM; OPEN QUESTION"
# The forms
plate: doc
dh: Household Return | REGISTRY FORM H-1 · AUREM
dsub: ONE COPY TO THE TEMPLE, ONE TO THE CROWN · ALL FIELDS REQUIRED
df: Household of | Parish, Celeste · Unbound
df: Adult male authority | "deceased" · returned with the word circled, next adult male requested
df: Dependents | ________________________________
df: Reclassification | six years pending
df: Authority, on resubmission | Zion Osei, eighteen
stamp: RETURNED
/end
rec:
The authority line | Household forms demand an adult male authority. Celeste wrote "deceased" on the line, and the form came back with the word circled and a request for the next adult male (DR3). Solomon's vocational form kept coming back to the house until Zion, at eighteen, declared himself the authority (TL, DR3).
Reclassifying | A woman reclassifying her household under her own registration has to prove the arrangement is permanent (TL). Celeste's took six years.
Moving | Moving between Dominions requires Crown authorization, technically available and practically difficult (MEN).
Citations | Any Ungoverned citation disqualifies a woman from the Covenant Registry (TR). OPEN QUESTION: What else does it do? Criminal record, designation, or both?
Deaths | Registered within 48 hours in the Crownward and 24 in Meridian (CR, DOM).
prompt: A form from the Osei household I want on the page, field by field | fill

@page id="p6-men" rh="BUILDING THE EMPIRE · VI · MEN, LABOR AND THE ECONOMY" m="TKC: MEN · DOM · TL"
# Control by structure
The Empire controls women through designation and men through structure. Men are told they're free, and the control sits in housing, careers and marriage approvals (MEN).
tabrl:
| Class | Who they are | Marriage |
| Common men | The labor and service class, registered to their Dominion | Expected by 21. Unmarried men past 22 stay in labor dormitories. Within caste and region. |
| Bonded men | Mid-tier administrators and skilled professionals, steered by Crown labor allocation | Between 21 and 25, from Unbound and Commonwife-eligible women, with Crown approval |
| Covenant men | Crown officers, Temple leadership, Broadcast directors, senior military | Between 25 and 35. Their wives are Anointed. |
| Conscript men | Common boys whose scores flag them. Officially they don't exist. | Crown approval at the Covenant Man level, for life |
rec:
The Imperial Education Track | A common boy whose Designation scores come back exceptional gets a visit from a Crown education officer within three months, offering a scholarship. The family can decline, and declining routes him to the worst labor for life. Accepting makes him Crown-adjacent for life (MEN). (Volume Two, Solomon.)
The Covenant Declaration | At 18, common men register a public statement of loyalty and readiness for service and marriage. Elite men register theirs at the end of the Track (MEN).
Twelve economies | Irongate manufacturing. Lone oil and space infrastructure. Mountain classified operations, extraction and military capacity. Gulf tourism, rebranded "cultural pilgrimage." Meridian law and administration. Vane the Broadcast (DOM).
small: INFERENCE: The forty percent of working-class jobs lost to automation in the Fracture Years never came back, which is what makes Imperial service look like opportunity to a common man.
prompt: How an Aurem common man gets authorization to move, and how often it's granted | fill

@page id="p6-doctrine" rh="BUILDING THE EMPIRE · VII · WHAT THE TEMPLE TEACHES" m="TKC: CR · TL · DR3 · TR"
# The official religion, in one line
plate: quote | | 2in
pk: Preached every week in every Temple
pq: The husband leads, the wife supports, the household reflects divine order in miniature.
pr: THE OFFICIAL THEOLOGY OF MARRIAGE (CR)
/end
rec:
The Vane anointing | God chose Elias to restore the nation, and the bloodline carries divine sanction (CR). Elias was canonized within a year, "not quite sainthood but close enough" (TL).
The House judges fairly | Bell tells Celeste "The House always judges fairly," and Celeste answers "The House calls whom it calls." Before the scores Bell speaks about "divine order, talent as stewardship, and elevation as burden," and closes with "May every daughter receive with grace the portion appointed to her" (DR3).
The House virtues | The banners in the Aurelia Temple atrium read obedience, discernment, modesty, stewardship and endurance (DR3).
Selection as revelation | The Broadcast frames the Trials as a revelation and never a competition: anointing, covenant, calling, worthy, chosen. A competition can be rigged, and a revelation can't (TR).
Being Bound as service | "Every girl in Aurem wanted to be Bound. It was the only way they knew how to serve." Covenant Candidacy is "the closest thing a woman could get to godliness" (DR3).
prompt: Which verses are carved over the doors of the Aurelia Temple and the Sanctum | fill

@page rh="BUILDING THE EMPIRE · VII · THE FOUNDING TEXTS" m="TKC: TL · AM · DR3; PART IV, THE SLAVE BIBLE"
# Four documents
rec:
*Thy Kingdom Come* · 2033 | Six hours, preached by Elias at The Gathering House. Required curriculum in every Dominion school since 2061 (TL). INTERPRETATION: The old companion reads it as a familiar prayer taken literally in public for the first time. Nobody in the stadium heard a coup. They heard their own prayer said aloud by a man who wasn't embarrassed by it (AM).
The Covenant of Purpose | A founding document. It describes a series of deliberate removals as God's order, and the east wing ledger describes the same removals as something else (TL). Celeste can recite it (AM).
The Covenant of Dissent | Written by Elias in the same hand and the same year, as the theological counter-argument to everything the Empire was built on. Catalogued until 2100, when it disappeared the same week as the ledger. Judah has been looking for it since before his brother's exile (TL).
The Book of Covenant | The book Bell reads from at the Assessment. It includes Proverbs 31.29, "Many daughters have done virtuously, but thou excellest them all," in King James wording (DR3). OPEN QUESTION: A full Protestant Bible, an abridged edition on the 1807 model, or scripture bound with the Empire's own founding texts?
tabrl:
| Passage | What the Assessment made of it (DR3) |
| Honor thy father and thy mother (Exod 20.12) | A question about reporting an Unbound woman who housed her children without male authority |
| The widow's oil (2 Kgs 4) | An argument for household rationing |
| Esther's crown | Proof a woman could influence government, "so long as she was beautiful, obedient, and prepared to die for the privilege" |
| Proverbs 31 | Printed on the first page and read before the scores |
small: Eden's read: "Facts might earn professional placement. Knowing which facts the House needed her to believe could carry her farther" (DR3).
prompt: The passage of the Covenant of Purpose about the removals, in the Empire's own voice | fill

@page rh="BUILDING THE EMPIRE · VII · LIVED RELIGION · 1 OF 6" m="TKC: DOM · MEN"
# What people actually practice
In every Dominion people do things around the official rites that the Empire doesn't know about, tolerates because stopping them would cost too much, or eventually approves because it already lost (DOM, MEN).
tabrl:
| Dominion | Practice | What it is | The Empire's position |
| Aurem | The Salt Ceremony | The bride's mother or grandmother washes her hands in salt water the night before her Binding and tells her the truth about marriage | Doesn't know it exists |
| Aurem | The old way of burial | Three days, family-led, no Empire officiant: the Telling, the Feeding, the Release. Red clay packed down by family hands. | Doesn't know. The paperwork is filed anyway. |
| Aurem | The Speaking | The night before a boy's Declaration, each man present tells him one true thing about being a man in Aurelia. The boy can't speak. | Unregulated |
| Aurem | Kitchens | Recipes that "looked like submission and tasted like memory" | Never thought the kitchen worth watching |
| Gulf | Music in worship | Live music at Bindings, against three ordinances, classed "ceremonial expression within approved parameters" | Tolerated for tourism money |
| Gulf | The Last Free Night | The women of the family dance, drink and tell the bride the truth | Never on paper |

prompt: Which of these my characters have done, seen or only heard about | rest
