@page id="p6-women" rh="BUILDING THE EMPIRE · VIII · THE DESIGNATIONS" m="TKC: CR · MEN · FR 1 · FR 19 · TR · DOM · TL"
# What each woman is
Since the Restoration Act of 2041, a woman's citizenship has depended on her marital status (TL). The Empire controls women by telling each one what she is and making that classification the frame of her whole life (MEN).
<div class="ladder">
<div class="rung pink">Anointed Wife<span>wife of a Covenant man · the Trials' product · lost at widowhood</span></div>
<div class="rung">Stewardwife<span>the Administrative Class</span></div>
<div class="rung">Bonded Wife<span>wife of a Bonded man (INFERENCE)</span></div>
<div class="rung">Commonwife<span>a registration status (FR 1) · quotas · Temple review</span></div>
<div class="rung vw">Vowed<span>Temple service · left by marriage</span></div>
<div class="rung vw">Unbound<span>unmarried · "difficult and surveilled"</span></div>
<div class="rung out">Ungoverned<span>outside the system · criminal since 2062</span></div>
</div>
@page rh="BUILDING THE EMPIRE · VIII · THE DESIGNATIONS, CONTINUED" m="TKC: CR · MEN · FR 1 · FR 19 · TR · DOM · TL"
# What each woman is, continued
rec:
The Widowed Covenant | Because the designation belongs to the marriage, an Anointed widow holds a temporary status for two years that keeps most protections. She's expected to remarry within the class or drop to Stewardwife. The senior Anointed Wives run a network so efficient that no widow has ever fallen below Stewardwife (CR).
The six | INFERENCE: Older notes describe a six-tier caste system. Leaving out the Ungoverned, the six are Anointed, Stewardwife, Bonded, Commonwife, Vowed and Unbound. OPEN QUESTION: Is that the six, what's the official order, and where does Vowed rank?
prompt: Can a woman move up, or only down? Where each door locks | fill

@page rh="BUILDING THE EMPIRE · VIII · BEING SORTED" m="TKC: DOM · DR3 · DECIDED"
# Designation
Every girl is assessed and given an initial classification. In Aurem it happens at eighteen, by a Vowed officer, testing literacy, theological knowledge and "domestic aptitude." The result is the Unbound track or the elevated track, which means better education and a chance at Covenant Daughter consideration (DOM).
tabrl:
| Dominion | When and how |
| Aurem | At 18, by a Vowed officer |
| Gulf | At 14, the Procession |
| Ardenne | Standard in Ashford. Hollow children tested when they can be brought in. |
| Meridian | Three-day sessions: theology, written reasoning, policy, "relational compliance" |
| Vane | At 16, the Broadcast Assessment, partly filmed |
| Irongate | At 14 the Placement, at 18 Imperial Designation |
| Heartland | At 13, the First Covenant, where her track is set in public |
| Red Earth | At 18, two weeks in Temple spaces, Crown facilities and "domestic simulation environments" |
| Lone | At 16 in Covenant Basin. The First Ride outside it. |
| Mountain | At 16, with a physical assessment. Girls chosen for classified Vowed service disappear from public life. |
| Sable | At 18 in Sable Reach. The Finding outside it. |
| Pacific | At 18. Every score reviewed in the Crownward for signs of inherited resistance. |
rec:
The Aurelia board | Administration, Education, Domestic Service, Housekeeping, Food Service, Binding Recommendation and Remedial Instruction. Strong doctrine can lead to Temple Administration, strong household judgment to a Binding recommendation, and girls who test well everywhere to professional placement (DR3).
Leah | DECIDED: Leah sat for an assessment. Bell offered to nominate her "for Home Instruction the following year" as a consolation (DR3). OPEN QUESTION: What is Home Instruction?
prompt: Leah's assessment day, from her side | fill

@page rh="BUILDING THE EMPIRE · VIII · THE BINDING" m="TKC: TL · DOM · DR3 · CR · AM"
# Marriage as designation
The Binding is marriage as a legal and theological designation (TL). In Aurem it's held in a Temple, witnessed by a Vowed officiant, and registered with the Crown and the Temple at once. The bride wears white and gold and takes a surname hyphenated with her husband's. Ministers sign Binding certificates beneath each husband's name (DR3).
plate: quote | | 1.9in
pk: The Aurem Binding
pq: Nobody asks if she consents. They ask if she is worthy. The correct answer is yes.
pr: DOM
/end
rec:
Across the Dominions | Meridian's is the most legally elaborate, with registration that can take three weeks. Red Earth's is the most surveilled, with Vowed witnesses at every one. Mountain adds Crown officers. Sable Reach binds at dawn. Gulf keeps its music. In the Crownward a Commonwife Binding is a short registration at the Crown office, and an Administrative Class Binding is recorded for the Broadcast (DOM, CR).
The Imperial Binding | Seven public stages: the Declaration, Covenant Week, the Anointing (a private ceremony on the sixth day in which the Prelate anoints the bride), the Binding Night, the Grand Binding (four hours in the Sanctum), the Procession down Sovereign Boulevard, and the Private Supper, five guests at most. The bride's vows are twice as long as the heir's. The Empire calls that theology. In practice it's a legal document spoken aloud in a sacred building (CR).
Names | OPEN QUESTION: Does the hyphenated surname apply only in Aurem? Celeste never took a Binding name, so she's still a Parish (AM). Miriam's would be something like Parish-Osei.
prompt: What an Aurem bride actually says when she's asked if she's worthy | fill

@page rh="BUILDING THE EMPIRE · VIII · COMMONWIVES, ANOINTED WIVES, THE ARRANGEMENT" m="TKC: DOM · TL · MEN · FR 0 · FR 3 · FR 16 · FR 19 · FR 28 · CR · TR"
# Two ends of the ladder
rec:
Commonwife marriage | How the Empire marries off the labor class. Aurem has the highest Commonwife rate, because the Empire made marriage look like the highest honor available there (DOM). A Commonwife is expected to conceive within the Covenant timeline. A Vowed officer conducts quarterly household assessments, and two years without a pregnancy brings informal comments first and then Temple review (MEN, TL). (Volume Two, Leah.)
Reassignment | When a husband abandons his Commonwife, she can be reassigned to another man's household. It happened to Miriam after Thomas left (FR 0). Thomas left voluntarily (FR 3). Celeste has no proof Miriam is alive (FR 16). OPEN QUESTION: Who decides it, what is her status during it, and why did Solomon keep the Osei name?
Anointed Wives | All thirty-nine Trials place Anointed wives, and the Crown contest is a layer on top. One woman goes to the heir, the other eleven are married down the elite ladder, and nobody goes home unassigned (FR 19). The husbands are chosen by the oversight committee and ranked by standing. A man can't reject the woman assigned to him, but he can decline the assignment altogether, which removes him from the ladder. It has happened twice (FR 28).
The Arrangement | Among the Anointed Court, male headship is mostly performance. Wives manage the information, relationships and social intelligence that make their husbands' positions work, and surface the right idea in private so the husband can present it in public. They never take credit or contradict him in public. New Anointed Wives learn it in the first year. Lyris runs the whole network, inherited from the Anointed Wife who mentored her. Celeste told Eden about the Arrangement before she left Aurem (CR).
small: INFERENCE: Ladies in Waiting, the third- and fourth-place finishers, stay in the Crownward in supporting roles (TR). Under FR 19 they'd also be married into elite households. Both hold if they're placed in Crownward households and serve the Anointed Wife too.
prompt: Who the two men were who declined their assignments, and what happened to them | fill

@page rh="BUILDING THE EMPIRE · VIII · THE HOUSEHOLD ON PAPER" m="TKC: TL · DR3 · FR 31 · FR 32 · DOM"
# Unbound, Ungoverned, and four households
rec:
Unbound | "Difficult and surveilled" (TL). An Unbound woman can head a household only through reclassification, and household paperwork still demands an adult male authority.
Children | The Empire classifies children by household registration, not parentage or appearance (FR 32). Hannah's daughter Naomi is eligible through Hannah's household and standing, whatever her father's status (FR 31).
The Ungoverned | Women who chose exile over designation. Their deepest roots are in the Ardenne hollows, where some communities have lived outside the system for two or three generations. The Empire calls them criminals, and the mountain people call them something closer to saints. Crossings happen through the Sable desert. Lone interprets "Ungoverned" loosely and never files a full report. Saint Meridian has the largest population of sympathizers, people inside the system who leave gaps in it on purpose (DOM).
tab:
| | Celeste's | Leah's | Hannah's | An Anointed household |
| On the paper | Unbound, reclassified. A young man on the authority line. | Commonwife | A former Vowed woman's | Covenant man, Anointed Wife |
